<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- v3.1 C1 — THE UPSTREAM RESOLUTION ORACLE (scaffold pom).

     Builds the driver that runs upstream's RELEASED rune-lang 9.83.0 Xtext
     pipeline in ITS OWN JVM and dumps every resolved cross-reference as TSV.
     That dump is the ground truth the fork's Layer-1 resolution spec is
     extracted against and pinned to.

     The build produces `target/classes` plus an UNSIGNED `target/lib` of COPIED
     runtime dependencies (maven-dependency-plugin `copy-dependencies`) — NOT an
     uber-jar. Shading was tried and retired; see the copy-vs-shade rationale on
     the plugin below, which is the reason the positive control exists.

     THE ORACLE LAW (inherited verbatim from scripts/holdout-oracle): the
     RELEASED 9.83.0 jars are the oracle — NEVER the vendored rune-dsl/ source.
     The vendored tree sits at 10.0.0-dev.8 and its grammar has REMOVED syntax
     9.83.0 still accepts (`regulatoryReference`, `using standard`, rule `as`
     identifiers, `segment` compat names, `label ... as`), so parsing corpus
     sources with it would fail on valid 9.83.0 input.
     Verified for THIS surface at C1: `git diff 9.83.0 HEAD` over the vendored
     tree leaves RosettaScopeProvider, types/, RosettaEcoreUtil and utils/
     byte-identical, so the vendored source is a faithful READING reference for
     the resolution rules even though it must never be the EXECUTING oracle.

     WHY OUT-OF-PROCESS, NOT A LIBRARY (the C1 finding). The fork and upstream
     both own the package `com.regnosys.rosetta.*` with incompatible shapes —
     e.g. `com.regnosys.rosetta.types.RType` is an INTERFACE in the fork and a
     CLASS upstream. Put both on one classpath and Guice dies with
     `IncompatibleClassChangeError: class ...RDataType has interface ...RType as
     super class`. In-process differential execution is therefore structurally
     impossible, not merely unwired. This is why the pre-existing
     XtextEquivalenceHarness never compared anything.

     TWO NON-OBVIOUS PINS, both measured on this box, both required:

     (1) THE COPIED JARS MUST BE STRIPPED OF THEIR SIGNATURES. Seven
         separately-signed Eclipse platform jars contribute classes to the
         package `org.eclipse.core.runtime` (equinox.common, osgi, core.runtime,
         core.jobs, equinox.registry, equinox.preferences, core.contenttype).
         The JVM rejects that with `SecurityException: class
         "org.eclipse.core.runtime.OperationCanceledException"'s signer
         information does not match signer information of other classes in the
         same package`. Removing META-INF/*.SF|DSA|RSA|EC is the fix; because
         the jars are copied rather than shaded, the driver does it per-jar in
         `target/lib` (strip-jar-signatures.py, run from run-oracle.sh) and
         never in the local Maven repository.

     (2) org.eclipse.emf.common MUST BE PINNED FORWARD. rune-lang 9.83.0's tree
         resolves emf.common to 2.30.0 (xtext 2.38.0's direct dep, nearest-wins)
         while emf.ecore lands at 2.42.0 via ecore.xmi 2.37.0. The newer ecore
         calls `CommonUtil.newURL(String)`, absent from 2.30.0, and the injector
         dies with NoSuchMethodError before parsing anything. 2.44.0 is pinned
         because it is the oldest emf.common JAR present in the local
         repository that carries the method (2.42.0 has only its POM cached, and
         this box gates OFFLINE). EMF patch level does not affect resolution
         semantics — it only has to be self-consistent enough to boot.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>org.finos.rune.oracle</groupId>
    <artifactId>rune-xtext-resolution-oracle</artifactId>
    <!-- Deliberately loud: this artifact is a local test instrument and must
         never appear in a release reactor or as anyone's dependency. -->
    <version>0.0.0.c1-oracle</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <rune-dsl.oracle.version>9.83.0</rune-dsl.oracle.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Pin (2) above. MUST precede rune-lang so nearest-wins takes it. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.eclipse.emf</groupId>
            <artifactId>org.eclipse.emf.common</artifactId>
            <version>2.44.0</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.finos.rune</groupId>
            <artifactId>rune-lang</artifactId>
            <version>${rune-dsl.oracle.version}</version>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!-- Dependencies are COPIED, not shaded. An uber-jar cannot carry
                 two files at the same path, and the Eclipse/EMF stack ships a
                 `plugin.properties` in almost every artifact — shading silently
                 kept one and dropped the rest, which broke EMF's diagnostic
                 message lookup (`_UI_DiagnosticRoot_diagnostic` MissingResource)
                 and made the VALIDATOR RETURN ZERO ISSUES ON EVERY INPUT. That
                 read exactly like upstream having no complaints. Copying the
                 jars keeps each one's resources intact; the signature stripping
                 the shade was there for is done per-jar by the driver. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.6.1</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>copy-deps</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals><goal>copy-dependencies</goal></goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}/lib</outputDirectory>
                            <includeScope>runtime</includeScope>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
